# Function reference: index

## About this notebook

This notebook follows ggplot2's [function reference](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/): its
sections and their descriptions, in its order, with each of its pages linked to the notebook that
follows that page's examples. Each notebook shows ggplot2's code for every example, draws with
Giggly the ones it can draw, and names what the rest are waiting for, so together they are also a
list of what remains. The descriptions are ggplot2's, about ggplot2.

Livebook opens a linked notebook as a notebook of its own. Each installs Giggly from the folder
above it, so open this one from a clone of the repository.

## Plot basics

All ggplot2 plots begin with a call to [`ggplot()`](ggplot.livemd), supplying default data and
aesthetic mappings, specified by [`aes()`](aes.livemd). You then add layers, scales, coords and
facets with `+`. To save a plot to disk, use [`ggsave()`](ggsave.livemd).

* [`ggplot()`](ggplot.livemd): Create a new ggplot
* [`aes()`](aes.livemd): Construct aesthetic mappings
* [`add_gg()` `%+%`](gg-add.livemd): Add components to a plot
* [`ggsave()`](ggsave.livemd): Save a ggplot (or other grid object) with sensible defaults
* [`qplot()` `quickplot()`](qplot.livemd): Quick plot

## Layers

### Geoms

A layer combines data, aesthetic mapping, a geom (geometric object), a stat (statistical
transformation), and a position adjustment. Typically, you will create layers using a `geom_`
function, overriding the default position and stat if needed.

* [`layer_geoms`](layer_geoms.livemd): Layer geometry display
* [`geom_abline()` `geom_hline()` `geom_vline()`](geom_abline.livemd): Reference lines: horizontal, vertical, and diagonal
* [`geom_bar()` `geom_col()` `stat_count()`](geom_bar.livemd): Bar charts
* [`geom_bin_2d()` `stat_bin_2d()`](geom_bin_2d.livemd): Heatmap of 2d bin counts
* [`geom_blank()`](geom_blank.livemd): Draw nothing
* [`geom_boxplot()` `stat_boxplot()`](geom_boxplot.livemd): A box and whiskers plot (in the style of Tukey)
* [`geom_contour()` `geom_contour_filled()` `stat_contour()` `stat_contour_filled()`](geom_contour.livemd): 2D contours of a 3D surface
* [`geom_count()` `stat_sum()`](geom_count.livemd): Count overlapping points
* [`geom_density()` `stat_density()`](geom_density.livemd): Smoothed density estimates
* [`geom_density_2d()` `geom_density_2d_filled()` `stat_density_2d()` `stat_density_2d_filled()`](geom_density_2d.livemd): Contours of a 2D density estimate
* [`geom_dotplot()`](geom_dotplot.livemd): Dot plot
* [`geom_function()` `stat_function()`](geom_function.livemd): Draw a function as a continuous curve
* [`geom_hex()` `stat_bin_hex()`](geom_hex.livemd): Hexagonal heatmap of 2d bin counts
* [`geom_freqpoly()` `geom_histogram()` `stat_bin()`](geom_histogram.livemd): Histograms and frequency polygons
* [`geom_jitter()`](geom_jitter.livemd): Jittered points
* [`geom_crossbar()` `geom_errorbar()` `geom_errorbarh()` `geom_linerange()` `geom_pointrange()`](geom_linerange.livemd): Vertical intervals: lines, crossbars & errorbars
* [`geom_map()`](geom_map.livemd): Polygons from a reference map
* [`geom_path()` `geom_line()` `geom_step()`](geom_path.livemd): Connect observations
* [`geom_point()`](geom_point.livemd): Points
* [`geom_polygon()`](geom_polygon.livemd): Polygons
* [`geom_qq_line()` `stat_qq_line()` `geom_qq()` `stat_qq()`](geom_qq.livemd): A quantile-quantile plot
* [`geom_quantile()` `stat_quantile()`](geom_quantile.livemd): Quantile regression
* [`geom_ribbon()` `geom_area()` `stat_align()`](geom_ribbon.livemd): Ribbons and area plots
* [`geom_rug()`](geom_rug.livemd): Rug plots in the margins
* [`geom_segment()` `geom_curve()`](geom_segment.livemd): Line segments and curves
* [`geom_smooth()` `stat_smooth()`](geom_smooth.livemd): Smoothed conditional means
* [`geom_spoke()`](geom_spoke.livemd): Line segments parameterised by location, direction and distance
* [`geom_label()` `geom_text()`](geom_text.livemd): Text
* [`geom_raster()` `geom_rect()` `geom_tile()`](geom_tile.livemd): Rectangles
* [`geom_violin()` `stat_ydensity()`](geom_violin.livemd): Violin plot
* [`coord_sf()` `geom_sf()` `geom_sf_label()` `geom_sf_text()` `stat_sf()`](ggsf.livemd): Visualise sf objects

### Stats

A handful of layers are more easily specified with a `stat_` function, drawing attention to the
statistical transformation rather than the visual appearance. The computed variables can be mapped
using [`after_stat()`](aes_eval.livemd).

* [`layer_stats`](layer_stats.livemd): Layer statistical transformations
* [`stat_ecdf()`](stat_ecdf.livemd): Compute empirical cumulative distribution
* [`stat_ellipse()`](stat_ellipse.livemd): Compute normal data ellipses
* [`geom_function()` `stat_function()`](geom_function.livemd): Draw a function as a continuous curve
* [`stat_identity()`](stat_identity.livemd): Leave data as is
* [`stat_summary_2d()` `stat_summary_hex()`](stat_summary_2d.livemd): Bin and summarise in 2d (rectangle & hexagons)
* [`stat_summary_bin()` `stat_summary()`](stat_summary.livemd): Summarise y values at unique/binned x
* [`stat_unique()`](stat_unique.livemd): Remove duplicates
* [`stat_sf_coordinates()`](stat_sf_coordinates.livemd): Extract coordinates from 'sf' objects
* [`stat_manual()`](stat_manual.livemd): Manually compute transformations
* [`stat_connect()`](stat_connect.livemd): Connect observations
* [`after_stat()` `after_scale()` `from_theme()` `stage()`](aes_eval.livemd): Control aesthetic evaluation

### Position adjustment

All layers have a position adjustment that resolves overlapping geoms. Override the default by using
the `position` argument to the `geom_` or `stat_` function.

* [`layer_positions`](layer_positions.livemd): Layer position adjustments
* [`position_dodge()` `position_dodge2()`](position_dodge.livemd): Dodge overlapping objects side-to-side
* [`position_identity()`](position_identity.livemd): Don't adjust position
* [`position_jitter()`](position_jitter.livemd): Jitter points to avoid overplotting
* [`position_jitterdodge()`](position_jitterdodge.livemd): Simultaneously dodge and jitter
* [`position_nudge()`](position_nudge.livemd): Nudge points a fixed distance
* [`position_stack()` `position_fill()`](position_stack.livemd): Stack overlapping objects on top of each another

### Annotations

Annotations are a special type of layer that don’t inherit global settings from the plot. They are
used to add fixed reference data to plots.

* [`geom_abline()` `geom_hline()` `geom_vline()`](geom_abline.livemd): Reference lines: horizontal, vertical, and diagonal
* [`annotate()`](annotate.livemd): Create an annotation layer
* [`annotation_custom()`](annotation_custom.livemd): Annotation: Custom grob
* [`annotation_logticks()`](annotation_logticks.livemd): Annotation: log tick marks
* [`annotation_map()`](annotation_map.livemd): Annotation: a map
* [`annotation_raster()`](annotation_raster.livemd): Annotation: high-performance rectangular tiling
* [`annotation_borders()` `borders()`](annotation_borders.livemd): Create a layer of map borders

## Aesthetics

The following help topics give a broad overview of some of the ways you can use each aesthetic.

* [`aes_colour_fill_alpha` `colour` `color` `fill`](aes_colour_fill_alpha.livemd): Colour related aesthetics: colour, fill, and alpha
* [`aes_group_order` `group`](aes_group_order.livemd): Aesthetics: grouping
* [`aes_linetype_size_shape` `linetype` `size` `shape`](aes_linetype_size_shape.livemd): Differentiation related aesthetics: linetype, size, shape
* [`aes_position` `x` `y` `xmin` `xmax` `ymin` `ymax` `xend` `yend`](aes_position.livemd): Position related aesthetics: x, y, xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax, xend, yend

## Scales

Scales control the details of how data values are translated to visual properties. Override the
default scales to tweak details like the axis labels or legend keys, or to use a completely
different translation from data to aesthetic. [`labs()`](labs.livemd) and [`lims()`](lims.livemd)
are convenient helpers for the most common adjustments to the labels and limits.

* [`labs()` `xlab()` `ylab()` `ggtitle()` `get_labs()`](labs.livemd): Modify axis, legend, and plot labels
* [`lims()` `xlim()` `ylim()`](lims.livemd): Set scale limits
* [`expand_limits()`](expand_limits.livemd): Expand the plot limits, using data
* [`expansion()` `expand_scale()`](expansion.livemd): Generate expansion vector for scales
* [`scale_alpha()` `scale_alpha_continuous()` `scale_alpha_binned()` `scale_alpha_discrete()` `scale_alpha_ordinal()`](scale_alpha.livemd): Alpha transparency scales
* [`scale_x_binned()` `scale_y_binned()`](scale_binned.livemd): Positional scales for binning continuous data (x & y)
* [`scale_colour_brewer()` `scale_fill_brewer()` `scale_colour_distiller()` `scale_fill_distiller()` `scale_colour_fermenter()` `scale_fill_fermenter()`](scale_brewer.livemd): Sequential, diverging and qualitative colour scales from ColorBrewer
* [`scale_colour_continuous()` `scale_fill_continuous()` `scale_colour_binned()` `scale_fill_binned()`](scale_colour_continuous.livemd): Continuous and binned colour scales
* [`scale_colour_discrete()` `scale_fill_discrete()`](scale_colour_discrete.livemd): Discrete colour scales
* [`scale_x_continuous()` `scale_y_continuous()` `scale_x_log10()` `scale_y_log10()` `scale_x_reverse()` `scale_y_reverse()` `scale_x_sqrt()` `scale_y_sqrt()`](scale_continuous.livemd): Position scales for continuous data (x & y)
* [`scale_x_date()` `scale_y_date()` `scale_x_datetime()` `scale_y_datetime()` `scale_x_time()` `scale_y_time()`](scale_date.livemd): Position scales for date/time data
* [`scale_x_discrete()` `scale_y_discrete()`](scale_discrete.livemd): Position scales for discrete data
* [`scale_colour_gradient()` `scale_fill_gradient()` `scale_colour_gradient2()` `scale_fill_gradient2()` `scale_colour_gradientn()` `scale_fill_gradientn()`](scale_gradient.livemd): Gradient colour scales
* [`scale_colour_grey()` `scale_fill_grey()`](scale_grey.livemd): Sequential grey colour scales
* [`scale_colour_hue()` `scale_fill_hue()`](scale_hue.livemd): Evenly spaced colours for discrete data
* [`scale_colour_identity()` `scale_fill_identity()` `scale_shape_identity()` `scale_linetype_identity()` `scale_linewidth_identity()` `scale_alpha_identity()` `scale_size_identity()` `scale_discrete_identity()` `scale_continuous_identity()`](scale_identity.livemd): Use values without scaling
* [`scale_linetype()` `scale_linetype_binned()` `scale_linetype_continuous()` `scale_linetype_discrete()`](scale_linetype.livemd): Scale for line patterns
* [`scale_linewidth()` `scale_linewidth_binned()`](scale_linewidth.livemd): Scales for line width
* [`scale_colour_manual()` `scale_fill_manual()` `scale_size_manual()` `scale_shape_manual()` `scale_linetype_manual()` `scale_linewidth_manual()` `scale_alpha_manual()` `scale_discrete_manual()`](scale_manual.livemd): Create your own discrete scale
* [`scale_shape()` `scale_shape_binned()`](scale_shape.livemd): Scales for shapes, aka glyphs
* [`scale_size()` `scale_radius()` `scale_size_binned()` `scale_size_area()` `scale_size_binned_area()`](scale_size.livemd): Scales for area or radius
* [`scale_colour_steps()` `scale_colour_steps2()` `scale_colour_stepsn()` `scale_fill_steps()` `scale_fill_steps2()` `scale_fill_stepsn()`](scale_steps.livemd): Binned gradient colour scales
* [`scale_colour_viridis_d()` `scale_fill_viridis_d()` `scale_colour_viridis_c()` `scale_fill_viridis_c()` `scale_colour_viridis_b()` `scale_fill_viridis_b()`](scale_viridis.livemd): Viridis colour scales from viridisLite
* [`get_alt_text()`](get_alt_text.livemd): Extract alt text from a plot

## Guides: axes and legends

The guides (the axes and legends) help readers interpret your plots. Guides are mostly controlled
via the scale (e.g. with the `limits`, `breaks`, and `labels` arguments), but sometimes you will
need additional control over guide appearance. Use [`guides()`](guides.livemd) or the `guide`
argument to individual scales along with `guide_*()` functions.

* [`draw_key_point()` `draw_key_abline()` `draw_key_rect()` `draw_key_polygon()` `draw_key_blank()` `draw_key_boxplot()` `draw_key_crossbar()` `draw_key_path()` `draw_key_vpath()` `draw_key_dotplot()` `draw_key_linerange()` `draw_key_pointrange()` `draw_key_smooth()` `draw_key_text()` `draw_key_label()` `draw_key_vline()` `draw_key_timeseries()`](draw_key.livemd): Key glyphs for legends
* [`guide_colourbar()` `guide_colorbar()`](guide_colourbar.livemd): Continuous colour bar guide
* [`guide_legend()`](guide_legend.livemd): Legend guide
* [`guide_axis()`](guide_axis.livemd): Axis guide
* [`guide_axis_logticks()`](guide_axis_logticks.livemd): Axis with logarithmic tick marks
* [`guide_axis_stack()`](guide_axis_stack.livemd): Stacked axis guides
* [`guide_axis_theta()`](guide_axis_theta.livemd): Angle axis guide
* [`guide_bins()`](guide_bins.livemd): A binned version of guide_legend
* [`guide_coloursteps()` `guide_colorsteps()`](guide_coloursteps.livemd): Discretized colourbar guide
* [`guide_custom()`](guide_custom.livemd): Custom guides
* [`guide_none()`](guide_none.livemd): Empty guide
* [`guides()`](guides.livemd): Set guides for each scale
* [`sec_axis()` `dup_axis()` `derive()`](sec_axis.livemd): Specify a secondary axis

## Facetting

Facetting generates small multiples, each displaying a different subset of the data. Facets are an
alternative to aesthetics for displaying additional discrete variables.

* [`facet_grid()`](facet_grid.livemd): Lay out panels in a grid
* [`facet_wrap()`](facet_wrap.livemd): Wrap a 1d ribbon of panels into 2d
* [`vars()`](vars.livemd): Quote faceting variables

### Labels

These functions provide a flexible toolkit for controlling the display of the “strip” labels on
facets.

* [`labeller()`](labeller.livemd): Construct labelling specification
* [`label_value()` `label_both()` `label_context()` `label_parsed()` `label_wrap_gen()`](labellers.livemd): Useful labeller functions
* [`label_bquote()`](label_bquote.livemd): Label with mathematical expressions

## Coordinate systems

The coordinate system determines how the `x` and `y` aesthetics combine to position elements in the
plot. The default coordinate system is Cartesian ([`coord_cartesian()`](coord_cartesian.livemd)),
which can be tweaked with [`coord_map()`](coord_map.livemd), [`coord_fixed()`](coord_fixed.livemd),
[`coord_flip()`](coord_flip.livemd), and [`coord_transform()`](coord_transform.livemd), or
completely replaced with [`coord_polar()`](coord_radial.livemd).

* [`coord_cartesian()`](coord_cartesian.livemd): Cartesian coordinates
* [`coord_fixed()`](coord_fixed.livemd): Cartesian coordinates with fixed "aspect ratio"
* [`coord_flip()`](coord_flip.livemd): Cartesian coordinates with x and y flipped
* [`coord_map()` `coord_quickmap()`](coord_map.livemd): Map projections
* [`coord_polar()` `coord_radial()`](coord_radial.livemd): Polar coordinates
* [`coord_transform()` `coord_trans()`](coord_transform.livemd): Transformed Cartesian coordinate system

## Themes

Themes control the display of all non-data elements of the plot. You can override all settings with
a complete theme like [`theme_bw()`](ggtheme.livemd), or choose to tweak individual settings by
using [`theme()`](theme.livemd) and the `element_` functions. Use [`theme_set()`](get_theme.livemd)
to modify the active theme, affecting all future plots.

* [`theme()`](theme.livemd): Modify components of a theme
* [`theme_grey()` `theme_gray()` `theme_bw()` `theme_linedraw()` `theme_light()` `theme_dark()` `theme_minimal()` `theme_classic()` `theme_void()` `theme_test()`](ggtheme.livemd): Complete themes
* [`get_theme()` `theme_get()` `set_theme()` `theme_set()` `update_theme()` `theme_update()` `replace_theme()` `theme_replace()` `%+replace%`](get_theme.livemd): Get, set, and modify the active theme
* [`theme_sub_axis()` `theme_sub_axis_x()` `theme_sub_axis_y()` `theme_sub_axis_bottom()` `theme_sub_axis_top()` `theme_sub_axis_left()` `theme_sub_axis_right()` `theme_sub_legend()` `theme_sub_panel()` `theme_sub_plot()` `theme_sub_strip()`](subtheme.livemd): Shortcuts for theme settings
* [`margin()` `margin_part()` `margin_auto()` `element()` `element_blank()` `element_rect()` `element_line()` `element_text()` `element_polygon()` `element_point()` `element_geom()` `rel()`](element.livemd): Theme elements

## Programming with ggplot2

These functions provides tools to help you program with ggplot2, creating functions and for-loops
that generate plots for you.

* [`aes_()` `aes_string()` `aes_q()`](aes_.livemd): Define aesthetic mappings programmatically
* [`print.ggplot` `plot.ggplot`](print.ggplot.livemd): Explicitly draw plot

## Extending ggplot2

To create your own geoms, stats, scales, and facets, you’ll need to learn a bit about the object
oriented system that ggplot2 uses. Start by reading
[`vignette("extending-ggplot2")`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/articles/extending-ggplot2.html)
then consult these functions for more details.

* [`ggproto()` `ggproto_parent()`](ggproto.livemd): Create a new ggproto object
* [`print(<ggproto>)` `format(<ggproto>)`](print.ggproto.livemd): Format or print a ggproto object

## Vector helpers

ggplot2 also provides a handful of helpers that are useful for creating visualisations.

* [`cut_interval()` `cut_number()` `cut_width()`](cut_interval.livemd): Discretise numeric data into categorical
* [`mean_cl_boot()` `mean_cl_normal()` `mean_sdl()` `median_hilow()`](hmisc.livemd): A selection of summary functions from Hmisc
* [`mean_se()`](mean_se.livemd): Calculate mean and standard error of the mean
* [`resolution()`](resolution.livemd): Compute the "resolution" of a numeric vector

## Data

ggplot2 comes with a selection of built-in datasets that are used in examples to illustrate various
visualisation challenges.

* [`diamonds`](diamonds.livemd): Prices of over 50,000 round cut diamonds
* [`economics` `economics_long`](economics.livemd): US economic time series
* [`faithfuld`](faithfuld.livemd): 2d density estimate of Old Faithful data
* [`midwest`](midwest.livemd): Midwest demographics
* [`mpg`](mpg.livemd): Fuel economy data from 1999 to 2008 for 38 popular models of cars
* [`msleep`](msleep.livemd): An updated and expanded version of the mammals sleep dataset
* [`presidential`](presidential.livemd): Terms of 12 presidents from Eisenhower to Trump
* [`seals`](seals.livemd): Vector field of seal movements
* [`txhousing`](txhousing.livemd): Housing sales in TX
* [`luv_colours`](luv_colours.livemd): [`colors()`](https://rdrr.io/r/grDevices/colors.html) in Luv space

## Autoplot and fortify

[`autoplot()`](autoplot.livemd) is an extension mechanism for ggplot2: it provides a way for package
authors to add methods that work like the base
[`plot()`](https://rdrr.io/r/graphics/plot.default.html) function, generating useful default plots
with little user interaction. [`fortify()`](fortify.livemd) turns objects into tidy data frames: it
has largely been superseded by the [broom package](https://github.com/tidyverse/broom).

* [`autoplot()`](autoplot.livemd): Create a complete ggplot appropriate to a particular data type
* [`autolayer()`](autolayer.livemd): Create a ggplot layer appropriate to a particular data type
* [`fortify()`](fortify.livemd): Fortify a model with data.
* [`map_data()`](map_data.livemd): Create a data frame of map data
* [`automatic_plotting`](automatic_plotting.livemd): Tailoring plots to particular data types
